The attached image (Fig. 3) demonstrates that Nano-11 induces a transient inflammatory response at the injection site and is taken up by recruited immune cells. Panel A, a line graph, shows a peak in Mac-2+ macrophages and antigen-presenting cells around Day 2, with neutrophils peaking earlier at Day 1, followed by a decrease in cell numbers by Day 21. Panel B displays confocal images illustrating intracellular Nano-11 (red) within various immune cells (MHCII+, Ly6C+, Mac-2+, 1A8+) at the injection site from 6 hours to Day 7, confirming that these cells efficiently phagocytize the nanoparticles. This figure supports Nano-11's ability to trigger local immune cell recruitment and antigen uptake, which resolves over time.